{title: 628 - As Jacob With Travel Was Weary}
{subtitle: SDA Hymnal #628 • JACOB'S LADDER • 11.11.11.12}
{lyricist: 18th century}
{composer: 18th century English carol melody}
{key: F}
{time: 4/4}
{tempo: Moderato}
{meta: hymn_number 628}
{meta: tune JACOB'S LADDER}
{meta: meter 11.11.11.12}
{meta: category Christian Life - }

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[C]As [F]Jacob with travel was [Gm]weary one day,
[Gm]At night [F]on a stone for a pillow [C]he lay;
[Am]He saw in a vision [C/G]a ladder [Bb]so high
[Am]That its [C]foot was on [Am]earth and [Gm]its top [F]in the sky.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus: Refrain}
[C]Alleluia [F]to Jesus who died [Gm]on the tree,
[Gm]And has [F]raised up a ladder of mercy [C]for me,
[Am]And has [C7]raised up a [Am]ladder [Gm]of mercy [F]for me.
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[C]Come [F]let us ascend! All may [Gm]climb it who will;
[Gm]For the [F]angels of Jacob are guarding [C]it still:
[Am]And remember each [C]step that by [Bb]faith we passo'er,
[Am]Some prophet [C]or martyr [Gm]has trod [F]it before.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[C]And [F]when we arrive at the [Gm]haven of rest
[Gm]We shall [F]hear the glad words, "Come up hither, [C]ye blest,
[Am]Here are regions of [C]light, here are [Bb]mansions of bliss."
[Am]O who [C]would not climb [Am]such [Gm]a ladder [F]as this?
{end_of_verse}
